   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Standing Versus Floor-Based Trunk Stability Training: Effects on Landing and Cutting Movement Quality in Youth Male Football Players</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Standing Versus Floor-Based Trunk Stability Training: Effects on Landing and Cutting Movement Quality in Youth Male Football Players</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>trunk stability; youth football; landing; cutting; change of direction; LESS; CMAS; neuromuscular training</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">This study compared the effects of a 5-week standing single-leg trunk-stability programme</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">This study compared the effects of a 5-week standing single-leg trunk-stability programme</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">a conventional floor-based trunk-stability programme</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">a conventional floor-based trunk-stability programme</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and active general conditioning on landing and cutting movement quality in early-adolescent male football players. In this exploratory three-arm randomised trial</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and active general conditioning on landing and cutting movement quality in early-adolescent male football players. In this exploratory three-arm randomised trial</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">31 early-adolescent male football players were allocated to a standing single-leg trunk-stability programme (n = 11)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">31 early-adolescent male football players were allocated to a standing single-leg trunk-stability programme (n = 11)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">a conventional floor-based trunk-stability programme (n = 12)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">a conventional floor-based trunk-stability programme (n = 12)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">or an active general-conditioning control programme (n = 8). Participants completed two supervised 15 min sessions per week. Co-primary outcomes were the Landing Error Scoring System (LESS) and bilateral mean Cutting Movement Assessment Score (CMAS); secondary outcomes were change-of-direction (COD) time and accelerometer-derived lumbopelvic stability. Baseline-adjusted ANCOVA showed significant group effects for LESS (F(2.27) = 17.02</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">or an active general-conditioning control programme (n = 8). Participants completed two supervised 15 min sessions per week. Co-primary outcomes were the Landing Error Scoring System (LESS) and bilateral mean Cutting Movement Assessment Score (CMAS); secondary outcomes were change-of-direction (COD) time and accelerometer-derived lumbopelvic stability. Baseline-adjusted ANCOVA showed significant group effects for LESS (F(2.27) = 17.02</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">p \textless  0.001</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">p \textless  0.001</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">$\upeta $p2 = 0.53) and CMAS (F(2.26) = 10.45</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">$\upeta $p2 = 0.53) and CMAS (F(2.26) = 10.45</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">p \textless  0.001</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">p \textless  0.001</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">$\upeta $p2 = 0.45). The standing single-leg group showed lower baseline-adjusted LESS and bilateral CMASs than both comparison groups in the primary analyses. The LESS finding remained robust after adjustment for age</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">$\upeta $p2 = 0.45). The standing single-leg group showed lower baseline-adjusted LESS and bilateral CMASs than both comparison groups in the primary analyses. The LESS finding remained robust after adjustment for age</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">whereas the CMAS pairwise contrasts were attenuated in the age-adjusted ANCOVA; however</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">whereas the CMAS pairwise contrasts were attenuated in the age-adjusted ANCOVA; however</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">the complementary age-adjusted side-specific analysis continued to support a group effect for CMAS. No clear between-group differences were observed for COD time or lumbopelvic acceleration. These exploratory findings support a task-specific effect of the standing single-leg programme on observable landing and cutting movement quality</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">the complementary age-adjusted side-specific analysis continued to support a group effect for CMAS. No clear between-group differences were observed for COD time or lumbopelvic acceleration. These exploratory findings support a task-specific effect of the standing single-leg programme on observable landing and cutting movement quality</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">although the CMAS result should be interpreted cautiously because the pairwise contrasts were attenuated after adjustment for chronological age.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">although the CMAS result should be interpreted cautiously because the pairwise contrasts were attenuated after adjustment for chronological age.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
